
Koko has several piles of bananas and must finish eating all of them within H hours. She chooses a constant eating speed K (bananas per hour). In each hour, she can eat up to K bananas from only one pile. If she finishes a pile before eating K bananas, she cannot start another pile in the same hour and must wait until the next hour. The goal is to find the minimum eating speed that allows her to finish all the bananas within the given number of hours

The minimum possible eating speed is 1 banana per hour, and the maximum possible eating speed is the size of the largest banana pile. If Koko chooses a speed equal to the largest pile, she can finish that entire pile in one hour, and every other pile will also take at most one hour. Therefore, this speed is guaranteed to be a valid solution. However, our goal is not just to find a valid speed, but the minimum valid eating speed. Hence, the answer lies in the range 1 to the maximum pile size, and we need to find the optimal speed within this range.

Brute Force

Try every possible eating speed from the minimum to the maximum. For each speed, scan all banana piles to calculate the total hours needed.

Binary Search

The answer has a monotonic property: if a certain eating speed works, then all higher speeds will also work. This is because increasing the eating speed can only reduce (or keep the same) the total number of hours required to finish all the banana piles. Once Koko can finish within H hours at a particular speed, any faster speed will also satisfy the condition. This creates a transition from invalid speeds to valid speeds, making binary search applicable.

Why Binary Search is Better

Instead of checking every possible speed, binary search repeatedly halves the search space and checks only a small number of candidate speeds. This makes it significantly more efficient than the brute force approach, especially when the maximum pile size is very large.


Let's say:

n = number of banana piles
m = maximum bananas in any pile
Brute Force Approach

In the brute force solution, you try every possible eating speed from 1 to m.

For each speed:

Traverse all n piles.
Calculate how many hours Koko needs at that speed.
Check whether the total hours is within the allowed limit.

So:

Number of possible speeds = m
Work done for each speed = n

Therefore,

Time Complexity = O(m × n)

In the binary search solution, you do not try every possible eating speed. Instead, you repeatedly choose the middle speed from the current range.

For each selected speed:

Traverse all n piles.
Calculate how many hours Koko needs at that speed.
Check whether the total hours are within the allowed limit.
Based on the result, eliminate half of the remaining speeds and continue searching in the appropriate half.

So:

Number of speeds checked = O(log m) (because the search space is halved in every iteration)
Work done for each speed = O(n) (scan all banana piles once)

Therefore,

Time Complexity = O(n × log m)

--------------------------------------------------------

class Solution {
public:
    int minEatingSpeed(vector<int>& piles, int h) {
        // Search space: eating speed can range from 1 to the largest pile
        int l = 1;
        int r = *max_element(piles.begin(), piles.end());

        // Binary search to find the minimum valid eating speed
        while (l <= r) {
            // Current eating speed
            int mid = l + (r - l) / 2;

            // Calculate total hours needed at this speed
            long long hours = 0;
            for (int pile : piles) {
                // Round up because a partially eaten pile still takes one full hour
                hours += ceil((double)pile / mid);
            }

            // If Koko can finish within h hours, try a smaller speed
            if (hours <= h)
                r = mid - 1;
            else
                // Otherwise, increase the eating speed
                l = mid + 1;
        }

        // l is the minimum eating speed that allows Koko to finish on time
        return l;
    }
};

ceil() only works correctly if it receives a floating-point value, which is why we convert pile to double.
